        <body>
    <h2>Requesting a Special Education Evaluation in NYC District 14</h2>

    <h3>Local Context: Williamsburg, Greenpoint, and Bushwick (North)</h3>
    <p>Navigating the special education system can be challenging. This guide provides specific information for parents and guardians in NYC District 14, encompassing the neighborhoods of Williamsburg, Greenpoint, and the northern sections of Bushwick.  We understand the unique needs of our diverse community, and we strive to ensure all children receive the appropriate support. This is especially important for families seeking a Bilingual IEP (Spanish, Polish, or Yiddish) or those within the Hasidic community accessing services through the Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE).</p>
    <p>District 14 serves a diverse population, including many families who speak languages other than English. It is essential that the evaluation process is culturally and linguistically appropriate. You have the right to request that evaluations are conducted in your child's native language (34 CFR §300.304(c)(1)). If you are a Spanish, Polish, or Yiddish-speaking family, explicitly request a bilingual evaluator in your initial written referral. This will ensure that the evaluation accurately reflects your child's abilities and needs without language barriers. The district is obligated to provide qualified interpreters and translators for all meetings and evaluations, as per NYS Part 200.5(d).</p>
    <p>For families within the Hasidic community accessing CPSE services, it's important to be aware of the unique cultural and religious considerations. Be sure to communicate these needs clearly to the CPSE and advocate for services that are sensitive to your family's traditions and values. This might include requesting providers who are familiar with the community and its customs.</p>

    <h3>Submitting a Written Referral for an Evaluation</h3>
    <p>The first step in obtaining special education services for your child is submitting a formal, written referral to the Committee on Special Education (CSE) Chairperson in District 14. This letter should clearly state your concerns about your child's academic, social-emotional, or physical development. Include specific examples of difficulties your child is experiencing at home, in school, or in the community. Be as detailed as possible. Your written referral should include your child's full name, date of birth, school, grade, your contact information (phone number and address), and a brief summary of your concerns. </p>
    <p>Consider requesting a meeting with your child’s teacher and/or school staff <i>before</i> submitting the referral. This can help you gather information and strengthen your referral letter.  Keep a copy of your referral letter for your records.</p>

    <p><b>What to Include in Your Referral Letter: A Detailed Checklist</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Child's Full Name and Date of Birth:</b> Double-check for accuracy.</li>
        <li><b>School and Grade:</b> State the current school and grade level.</li>
        <li><b>Your Contact Information:</b> Provide a reliable phone number and email address where you can be easily reached.  Include your physical address.</li>
        <li><b>Specific Concerns:</b> This is the most crucial part. Be as detailed as possible.
            <ul>
                <li><b>Academic Concerns:</b> Are there difficulties with reading, writing, math, or specific subjects? Provide examples of specific assignments where your child struggled. Include information about grades, test scores, and classroom performance.</li>
                <li><b>Social-Emotional Concerns:</b> Is your child experiencing anxiety, depression, or difficulty interacting with peers? Describe specific behaviors you have observed. Has there been any bullying or other negative social interactions?</li>
                <li><b>Behavioral Concerns:</b> Is your child exhibiting disruptive or challenging behaviors in the classroom or at home? Provide specific examples, including the frequency and duration of these behaviors.</li>
                <li><b>Physical Concerns:</b> Are there concerns about your child's motor skills, coordination, or physical health that may be impacting their learning? Include any relevant medical information or diagnoses.</li>
                <li><b>Communication Concerns:</b> Does your child have difficulty expressing themselves or understanding others?  Note any speech delays or language impairments.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
       <li><b>Developmental History:</b> Include any relevant information about your child's developmental milestones, such as when they started walking, talking, and reading.</li>
       <li><b>Medical History:</b> List any significant medical conditions, diagnoses, or medications your child is taking.</li>
       <li><b>Previous Interventions:</b> Describe any interventions or supports that have already been tried, such as tutoring, counseling, or behavioral interventions.  Include the results of these interventions.</li>
       <li><b>Language Needs:</b> Clearly state if your child requires a bilingual evaluation or services in a specific language (e.g., Spanish, Polish, Yiddish).</li>
       <li><b>Request for Evaluation:</b> Clearly state that you are requesting a special education evaluation to determine if your child is eligible for special education services.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Sample Referral Letter Excerpt:</b></p>
    <p><i>"I am writing to request a special education evaluation for my son, [Child's Name], who is in [Grade] at [School Name]. I am concerned about his reading comprehension. He struggles to understand what he reads, even when the material is at his grade level. For example, on a recent reading comprehension test, he scored a 60%, and he frequently gets frustrated when asked to read aloud. At home, he avoids reading and often asks for help with simple tasks that require reading. I believe that a special education evaluation is necessary to determine the underlying cause of his reading difficulties and to identify appropriate interventions and supports."</i></p>

    <h3>Important: The 60-Day Timeline</h3>
    <p>Be aware that once the CSE receives your written referral, they have a legal obligation to determine whether to evaluate your child within 60 school days (excluding summer recess and school breaks). This timeline includes the initial evaluation, the Eligibility Determination Meeting, and the development of an Individualized Education Program (IEP) if your child is found eligible for special education services.  It's crucial to track this timeline and communicate with the CSE Chairperson if you have any concerns about delays.  Document all communication with the CSE.</p>

    <p><b>Breaking Down the 60-Day Timeline:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Day 1:</b> The CSE receives your written referral. This is Day 1 of the 60-school-day timeline. Obtain confirmation of receipt from the CSE (email or written acknowledgment).</li>
        <li><b>Within 30 School Days (NYS Part 200.4(a)(1)(i)):</b> The CSE must decide whether to evaluate your child. They will review your referral and may request additional information from you or the school. They will send you a Prior Written Notice (PWN) indicating their decision to evaluate or not evaluate. If they decide *not* to evaluate, they must provide a clear explanation of the reasons why, as required by IDEA 2004. You have the right to challenge this decision through mediation or a due process complaint.</li>
        <li><b>If the CSE decides to evaluate:</b> You will receive a consent form outlining the proposed evaluations. It is *critical* to carefully review this consent form. You have the right to request specific evaluations or to decline certain evaluations (34 CFR §300.304(c)(4)). For example, if you believe an independent psychological evaluation is necessary, you can request that the district conduct one or provide funding for you to obtain one independently (see below for more on IEEs). Return the signed consent form to the CSE promptly.</li>
        <li><b>Evaluations Conducted:</b> The CSE will schedule and conduct the evaluations. These evaluations may include psychological, educational, speech-language, occupational therapy, and physical therapy evaluations, depending on your child's needs. Ensure that all evaluations are conducted by qualified professionals.</li>
        <li><b>Eligibility Determination Meeting:</b> After the evaluations are completed, the CSE will schedule an Eligibility Determination Meeting to review the evaluation results and determine if your child meets the criteria for special education services under one of the thirteen disability categories defined in the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). You are a crucial member of the CSE team.</li>
        <li><b>IEP Development (If Eligible):</b> If your child is found eligible for special education services, the CSE will develop an Individualized Education Program (IEP) tailored to your child's specific needs. This IEP will outline your child's goals, services, and accommodations.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><b>What to Do If the 60-Day Timeline is Not Met:</b></p>
    <p>If the CSE fails to meet the 60-day timeline, take the following steps:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep a detailed record of all communication with the CSE, including dates, times, and the content of conversations.</li>
        <li><b>Contact the CSE Chairperson:</b> Send a written (email is acceptable) reminder to the CSE Chairperson, referencing the 60-day timeline and requesting an update on the evaluation process.</li>
        <li><b>Contact the District 14 Special Education Office:</b> If you do not receive a satisfactory response from the CSE Chairperson, contact the District 14 Special Education Office (check the DOE website for current contact information).</li>
        <li><b>File a Complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED):</b> If the delays persist, you have the right to file a formal complaint with NYSED. Information on how to file a complaint can be found on the NYSED website.</li>
        <li><b>Consider Mediation or Due Process:</b> If you are unable to resolve the issue through informal channels, you may consider mediation or filing a due process complaint. These are more formal legal processes that can help to resolve disputes with the school district.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><b>Understanding Prior Written Notice (PWN)</b></p>

    <p>Prior Written Notice (PWN) is a critical component of the special education process, ensuring that parents are informed of proposed or refused actions by the school district regarding their child's education. According to IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act), the school district must provide you with PWN whenever they propose to initiate or change, or refuse to initiate or change, the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child, or the provision of a free appropriate public education (FAPE) to your child (34 CFR §300.503).</p>

    <p><b>What Must Be Included in a PWN:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Description of the Action:</b> A clear explanation of what the school district proposes or refuses to do.</li>
        <li><b>Reasons for the Action:</b> The reasons why the school district is taking this action.</li>
        <li><b>Description of Other Options Considered:</b> A description of other options that were considered and the reasons why those options were rejected.</li>
        <li><b>Description of Each Evaluation Procedure, Test, Record, or Report Used:</b> A description of the evaluation procedures, tests, records, or reports that the school district used as a basis for the proposed or refused action.</li>
        <li><b>Sources to Contact for Assistance:</b> A description of any other factors that are relevant to the school district's proposal or refusal.</li>
        <li><b>Parent Rights:</b> A statement informing you of your rights under IDEA and how to obtain a copy of the procedural safeguards.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Why PWN is Important:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Informed Decision-Making:</b> PWN provides you with the information you need to make informed decisions about your child's education.</li>
        <li><b>Transparency:</b> PWN ensures that the school district is transparent about its decisions and the reasons behind them.</li>
        <li><b>Accountability:</b> PWN holds the school district accountable for its actions and ensures that it is following the requirements of IDEA.</li>
        <li><b>Dispute Resolution:</b> PWN provides a basis for resolving disputes with the school district. If you disagree with the school district's proposed or refused action, you can use the PWN to understand the school district's reasoning and to prepare your response.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>What to Do When You Receive a PWN:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Read it Carefully:</b> Take the time to read the PWN carefully and make sure you understand what it says.</li>
        <li><b>Ask Questions:</b> If you have any questions about the PWN, contact the CSE or school district for clarification.</li>
        <li><b>Seek Advice:</b> Consider seeking advice from a special education attorney or advocate to help you understand your rights and options.</li>
        <li><b>Respond:</b> If you disagree with the school district's proposed or refused action, respond in writing to the PWN, explaining your concerns and providing any supporting documentation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>

    <p>Parents have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) of their child at public expense if they disagree with an evaluation conducted by the school district (34 CFR §300.502). An IEE is an evaluation conducted by a qualified examiner who is not employed by the school district. This is a powerful tool for parents who feel the district's evaluations are inadequate or biased.</p>

    <p><b>When to Consider Requesting an IEE:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>You Disagree with the District's Evaluation Results:</b> If you believe the district's evaluation did not accurately assess your child's strengths and needs.</li>
        <li><b>You Believe the Evaluator Was Not Qualified:</b> If you have concerns about the qualifications or experience of the evaluator who conducted the district's evaluation.</li>
        <li><b>The Evaluation Was Incomplete:</b> If you believe the district's evaluation did not assess all areas of concern.</li>
        <li><b>You Suspect Bias:</b> If you believe the district's evaluation was biased in any way.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>How to Request an IEE at Public Expense:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Notify the CSE in Writing:</b> Send a written request to the CSE Chairperson, stating that you disagree with the district's evaluation and are requesting an IEE at public expense. Clearly state the reasons for your disagreement.</li>
        <li><b>The District's Options:</b> The district has two options:
            <ul>
                <li><b>Fund the IEE:</b> The district can agree to fund the IEE and provide you with a list of qualified evaluators.</li>
                <li><b>Request a Due Process Hearing:</b> The district can request a due process hearing to demonstrate that its evaluation was appropriate.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b> If the district requests a due process hearing, they must prove that their evaluation was appropriate. If the hearing officer finds that the district's evaluation was appropriate, you still have the right to obtain an IEE, but you will have to pay for it yourself.</li>
        <li><b>IEE Standards:</b> If the IEE is at public expense (either funded by the district or ordered by a hearing officer), the IEE must meet the same standards as the district's evaluations. The evaluator must be qualified, and the evaluation must be conducted in accordance with professional standards.</li>
        <li><b>IEE Results:</b> The results of the IEE must be considered by the CSE in making decisions about your child's special education services. The IEE can also be presented as evidence at a due process hearing.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What if my child already has a diagnosis from a private doctor?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: While a private diagnosis can be helpful, the CSE is still required to conduct its own evaluations to determine eligibility for special education services. The CSE will consider the information provided by your doctor, but they will also conduct their own assessments to determine your child's educational needs.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can I bring an advocate or attorney to the CSE meetings?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: Yes, you have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to any CSE meeting. An advocate or attorney can help you understand your rights, navigate the special education process, and advocate for your child's needs.  Under IDEA, you have the right to be accompanied and advised by counsel and by individuals with special knowledge or expertise regarding your child (34 CFR §300.321(a)(6)).</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the IEP that the CSE develops?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: If you disagree with the IEP, you have several options:
            <ul>
                <li><b>Discuss Your Concerns:</b> First, try to discuss your concerns with the CSE and see if you can reach an agreement.</li>
                <li><b>Request Mediation:</b> You can request mediation, which is a process where a neutral third party helps you and the school district reach an agreement.</li>
                <li><b>File a Due Process Complaint:</b> You can file a due process complaint, which is a formal legal process where a hearing officer will make a decision about the IEP.</li>
                <li><b>Unilaterally Implement Services:</b> In some cases, you may choose to unilaterally implement services and seek reimbursement from the school district. However, this is a complex legal issue, and you should consult with an attorney before taking this step.</li>
            </ul>
        </dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What are my rights if my child is suspended or disciplined?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: If your child has an IEP, they have additional protections under IDEA regarding suspension and discipline. If your child is suspended for more than 10 days, the school district must conduct a manifestation determination to determine if the behavior was related to their disability. If the behavior was related to their disability, the school district must take steps to address the underlying cause of the behavior. You have the right to challenge any disciplinary action that you believe is unfair or discriminatory.</dd>
            
        <dt><b>Q: What if my child needs assistive technology?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: Assistive technology (AT) is any device or service that helps a student with a disability to access their education. If you believe your child needs AT, you should request that the CSE conduct an AT assessment. The IEP team will then determine if AT is necessary for your child to receive a free appropriate public education (FAPE).  This is specifically addressed in IDEA (34 CFR §300.5).</dd>
    </dl>
</body>
